no more anti-racism protests have been  held across the United Kingdom as part  of the black lives matter movement the  latest wave started in the US after the  killing of George Floyd three weeks ago  but since had spread around the world  one of those to take part was Rose  Hudson Wilkin the Bishop of Dover and  the first black woman bishop in the  Church of England's history just have a  quick listen to some of what she had to  say yesterday and we have been dying  we have been dying in so many ways and  we have been dying in a way that slowly  has also killed the next generation you  can put a stop to it we can put a stop  to it so please let's do that and let's  do it peacefully we just had a little  piece of say sorry to say that we're now  joined by Rose Hudson will conveying on  the program miss Hudson will Kim what  made you decide to speak out at the  protest yesterday you know these were  young people who had organized this  protest march and I felt that the church  I needed to be there with them I  couldn't let them do this on my behalf  or on their own we needed to stand  peacefully together you compared racism  to you coronavirus as saying we've been  dying in a way that slowly has also  killed the next generation do you think  the UK is a racist country do you know I  would be I would be lying if I did not  say that racism exists in this country  as it exists in other countries - this  is not a UK thing this is not an America  thing this is a world thing people who  are black have been experiencing this  kind of injustice for such a long time  and it is about time now as we move into  this century for us all to say that this  is unacceptable this is what is  unacceptable and we must no longer clue  with our silence or pretend it doesn't  matter to me I'm all right jack  we are not all right if one part of  humanity is not okay is there anything  specifically that you would like to see  happen as a result of the protest that  we've seen yes what I want to see is the  kind of social transformation breaking  down structural racism I want every  institution to look at itself I want  them to ask questions like why have we  not got people of all different  backgrounds cultural backgrounds in  leadership within these institutions you  see for too long we have been content in  our organizations in our institutions  and take me for granted but black people  we can do the cleaning we can do the the  work in the kitchen but we cannot sit at  the table for too long we have looked at  black people and said actually they  don't have leadership qualities No  enough is enough we are just as good as  anyone else and as al Sharpton said in  one of his memorial addresses the world  has had its its knees on our necks  preventing us from breathing preventing  the very life that God has given us from  rising and thriving enough is enough  so I want government please no more  reports I want action and the people who  are protesting black and white get if  you look at the groups protesting it's  not this is not a black thing the whole  community is saying enough is enough  I want your organization I wanted the  the media generally I want every  institution including the church to ask  the questions and to make it right  some pretty powerful words there and  some soul-searching no nothing least go  on for many of us you mentioned the  church there now that John sent some you  the former Archbishop of York has set  down the five most senior people in the  church are all white but acceptable it's  not acceptable and you know I you know I  love the Church of which I am a part but  I am NOT going to sit here and tell you  that it's alright it is not okay and so  we to ask the church we must examine  ourselves and it is not good enough for  us to make pronouncements about other  organizations we too must examine  ourselves and can I just very briefly  also say one of the things I've heard  being said is that our generation cannot  make statements about what happened in  the past we cannot speak for or judge  the past by our standards you know I  wonder if I really hearing what you are  saying because in effect what we are  saying is we're saying something about  the moral impoverishment of those who  have gone before us that they would have  thought it was okay to put up statues  and applaud people who had committed  acts of violence against humanity so we  are right today to say that shouldn't  happen I personally don't want those  plaques to be taken away  I want those those statues to be taken  away I would love to see a plaque  written up that tells the true story so  that history can really be told and in  the right way you take it away and it  gets forgotten but just the privileged  few who is able to go into a museum so  that's just my take on that particular  aspect but we have arrived today to make  statements about things that has  happened in the past why because it is  actually impacting on us today do you  know what people say to me I've had  letters of people telling me if I don't  like what's  happening in this country to go home and  they've also said look at you people  look how you're knifing each other let  me tell you something let me tell this  to all of Britain the reason why we're  having those situations on our streets  is because for too long our children  have been told that they are of no worth  that they have no value and they have  begun to believe this for themselves and  that is why they have been destroying  themselves we need to change our society  need to be they need to change and we  need to be the change that we want to  see so I applaud those who are  peacefully standing up to say we want a  different world to live in  just finally you've been a trailblazer  yourself really and you were the first  female black bishop you were day into  the priest in 1994 just a month after it  was possible for women to do so and  you've spoken a little bit about some  racism that you've experienced yourself  saying people rejected you for example  for doing their funerals because you  were black what kind of experiences have  you had and what advice would you give  to others who may be experiencing racism  in their own lives I want to tell you  that I am I'm lucky that I was born and  brought up in Jamaica because growing up  there I my Who I am was cemented I knew  I was somebody so when I came to this  country and people tried to treat me as  if I was a nobody I knew that they were  wrong now you contrast that with someone  who is born and brought up here who do  not have images of themselves in all  walks of life they don't know they don't  know that they are a child of God and  actually they don't need to to  internalize that and so I would say to  those who who are experiencing racism in  a way that impacts on their lives that  you know I long for them to know that  they are special that they are human and  and not to internalize other people's  negativity it belongs to them they  to be healed from its to do not become a  victim it is their problem  unfortunately it impacts on us but we  need to find a way that we can show our  resilience and I come from a people who  are resilient as black people we are  resilient when you think of all that's  happening to us in our history and yet  we can still smile we can still sing we  can still stand there our rhythm our  dance will not be taken away from us we  will still stand and we will seek  justice but not just justice for one  group of people but justice for all  because that is how we're going to live  in harmony with one another black and  white pink and blue young and old  together thank you so much some really  powerful words there as usual Rose  Hudson welcome thank you for being on  the program thank you
 

